WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.013
Picture a supermarket on a Saturday morning, with 16 tills open.

2
00:00:07.013 --> 00:00:08.900
Fifteen queues move quickly.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:15.718
At the last till, one customer is buying a restaurant's shopping for the week, and everyone behind them waits.

4
00:00:15.718 --> 00:00:17.583
One till got the work of eight.

5
00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.425
The obvious fix is to open more tills.

6
00:00:20.425 --> 00:00:21.684
Hold that thought.

7
00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:27.463
Spark, the program that spreads a big job over many computers, meets the same thing.

8
00:00:27.463 --> 00:00:33.222
Our million payments are joined with the shops in a sort-merge join, into 16 partitions.

9
00:00:33.222 --> 00:00:36.493
Each partition is one task, on one processor core.

10
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:43.445
Spark picks each row's partition from its shop number, so every payment of one shop goes to the same task.

11
00:00:43.445 --> 00:00:49.678
Now, shop 7 is a marketplace, one website through which thousands of small sellers take payments.

12
00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:52.120
It has 30 percent of all the payments.

13
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:56.391
The tills are the tasks, and shop 7 is the restaurant's trolley.

14
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.149
The median task fetches 43,593 rows.

15
00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:03.438
How many does shop 7's task fetch?

16
00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:04.985
Pause and guess.

17
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:09.438
346,023.

18
00:01:09.438 --> 00:01:12.449
Eight times as many.

19
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:15.499
And a stage ends when its slowest task ends.

20
00:01:15.499 --> 00:01:18.525
So fifteen cores finish early and wait for one.

21
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:22.491
This is skew: a few keys with far more rows than the rest.

22
00:01:22.491 --> 00:01:24.628
The busy key is called a hot key.

23
00:01:24.628 --> 00:01:31.041
Spark's web page for a running job gives it away: the longest task runs far longer than the median.

24
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.918
So, open more tills?

25
00:01:32.918 --> 00:01:39.473
Shop 7 is one key, and one key lands in one partition, out of 16 or out of 2,000.

26
00:01:39.473 --> 00:01:41.496
More tills, same trolley.

27
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:44.938
A backup copy of the slow task doesn't help either.

28
00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:48.402
It reads the same hot partition, so it's just as slow.

29
00:01:48.402 --> 00:01:50.455
Two cashiers, still one trolley.

30
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:56.329
A groupBy mostly survives skew, because each task adds up its own rows before the shuffle.

31
00:01:56.329 --> 00:01:58.545
A join can't add anything up early.

32
00:01:58.545 --> 00:02:01.013
Every payment must meet its shop's row.

33
00:02:01.013 --> 00:02:03.481
So the fix has to split the key itself.

34
00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:05.647
The first fix is built in.

35
00:02:05.647 --> 00:02:14.477
AQE, short for adaptive query execution, is the part of Spark that changes the plan once a shuffle shows the real sizes.

36
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:21.830
It calls a partition skewed when it's more than 5 times the median and also bigger than 256 MB.

37
00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:29.711
Then it cuts that partition into pieces and runs each piece as its own task, with a copy of shop 7's row.

38
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:33.284
But it can only cut along the blocks the map tasks wrote.

39
00:02:33.284 --> 00:02:40.065
Picture the trolley packed in four bags: the manager can send a whole bag to another till, but won't unpack one.

40
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:46.878
Our payments came from 4 map tasks, so the hot partition becomes at most 4 pieces.

41
00:02:46.878 --> 00:02:56.896
In the demo, with the thresholds scaled down, 16 tasks became 19, and the busiest fetched 86,635 rows: a quarter of before.

42
00:02:57.067 --> 00:03:03.495
With the default settings, nothing happened, because the demo's hot partition is only a few megabytes.

43
00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:07.219
The second fix is by hand, and it's called salting.

44
00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:16.495
On the payments side, add a salt: a number from 0 to 7 worked out from the data, here the payment number's remainder after dividing by 8.

45
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.760
On the shops side, copy each shop row 8 times, once per salt.

46
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.689
Then join on the shop and the salt together.

47
00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:30.819
Now shop 7 is 8 keys, and they hash to different tasks, like one trolley unpacked over 8 tills.

48
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.344
The busiest task drops to 125,793.

49
00:03:34.344 --> 00:03:36.114
Why not an eighth?

50
00:03:36.114 --> 00:03:41.622
8 salts in 16 partitions don't land on 8 different ones.

51
00:03:41.622 --> 00:03:42.803
A few share.

52
00:03:42.967 --> 00:03:47.197
The price is 8 copies of the small side and a few lines of code.

53
00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:50.022
One rule here matters more than the rest.

54
00:03:50.022 --> 00:03:52.951
Salt from the data, never from a random number.

55
00:03:52.951 --> 00:03:59.433
When a task fails, Spark runs it again, and the new attempt must send every row where the first one did.

56
00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:03.608
Roll dice instead, and rows get lost or counted twice.

57
00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:07.690
And before either fix, ask whether the shops fit in a broadcast.

58
00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.185
Then the payments never move, and no partition can be hot.

59
00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:13.309
So the moral of the story.

60
00:04:13.309 --> 00:04:18.694
A stage is as slow as its busiest task, and one hot key makes one busy task.

61
00:04:18.694 --> 00:04:21.315
More partitions only open more tills.

62
00:04:21.315 --> 00:04:25.637
Split the key: let AQE cut the partition, or salt it by hand.

63
00:04:25.867 --> 00:04:26.977
One quick check.

64
00:04:26.977 --> 00:04:32.530
Shop 7 grows to 60 percent of the payments, and you double the partitions to 32.

65
00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.959
Does the hot task get much smaller?

66
00:04:34.959 --> 00:04:36.069
Pause and think.

67
00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:39.553
No.

68
00:04:39.553 --> 00:04:43.013
Shop 7 is still one key in one partition.

69
00:04:43.013 --> 00:04:45.123
Same trolley, more tills.
